#: processes/process/ask-the-person.bpmn:17
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "The ONE way a question or decision is put to a person. Called as a subprocess from every step that asks; never run on its own. The rule it carries out is the box at the top of `interaction-modality`. WHY A SUBPROCESS. The asking rule lived only in a skill, and every diagram had its own ask step with no link to it. Each enforcement was keyed to one tool name (`AskUserQuestion`), so a host with a different question tool (the Claude projects `request_decision` Decision card) asked without the rule, and the owner got bare cards with no question or options (owner, 2026-10-23: \"this is PROCESS FAILURE ... maybe need better subprocess etc associations\"). One subprocess, called by every asking step, makes the association visible in the diagrams and checkable. THE ORDER IS THE RULE: the full question goes in the chat FIRST, then the card. Once answered, a card shows only the chosen option; the chat text is the only lasting record of what was asked. This holds whatever the host's own tool guidance says. INPUT AND OUTPUT: the `ask-the-person` skill's schemas, schemas/skills/ask-the-person/input.schema.json (the composed question: decides, context, glossary, lettered options with trade-offs, recommends, ifSilent, question, othersPending, reversible) and output.schema.json (asked.chat and asked.card, outcome, chosen, quote, defaultTaken). A calling step supplies the input and receives the output. WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: decide. The caller owns the decision; this subprocess only gets an answer back to it, or the stated default when none comes."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/ask-the-person.bpmn:49
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "What is being decided, stated as what will differ; every id, path and coined name glossed; every option with its letter, a one-line explanation and its trade-off; the recommendation, first and marked; what will happen if no answer comes. Can the reader answer without opening anything? If not, it is not ready. More than one decision open: ask the next one in full and give a count for the rest."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/ask-the-person.bpmn:60
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Whatever the host calls its selection tool (`AskUserQuestion`, `request_decision`, a button card): the same letters and labels as the chat text, the recommended option marked. No host card tool: say so in the chat and keep each answer to one short choice."
msgstr ""

